#
# prima-materia — the searches of the run on the Yasenkanna, 2026-10-16
#
# In a file of their own for the mechanical reason recorded at
# examinations/searches-jain.ttl and in prima-materia#703.
#

pmp:JapaneseZenKikenSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Every caution, danger and named failure in the Yasenkanna"@en, "Jede Warnung, Gefahr und benannte Misslingensform im Yasenkanna"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Hakuin, Yasenkanna, preface and main text, read complete in the Japanese of Shaku Dokutan's edition, Hakuin zenji hōgoroku, pp. 12-33; every passage found checked in Norman Waddell's translation (The Eastern Buddhist XXXIV, 1) and, for the main text, in Trevor Leggett's."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "Any statement that a practice taught or reported in the work can harm the practitioner, or fail to bring about what it is practised for, and any account of why."@en ;
    pm:searchTerms "In the Japanese: 禪病, 重症, 重病, 難治, 發狂, 心火逆上, 肺金, 度に過ぎ, 節を失し, 多觀, 邪觀, 滯碍, 守屍鬼, 頑空, 起つ事能はじ, 起つ事得じ, 功を立つる事能はず, 寸功なし, 遲速, 精麤; in the English of both translations at the same places: Zen sickness, illness, madness, heart-fire, too hard, false meditation, stagnate, corpse-guarding, never recover, no effect, swiftly or slowly."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:JapaneseZenZenbyo, pmc:JapaneseZenHakkyo, pmc:JapaneseZenShushiki, pmc:JapaneseZenTaitai, pmc:JapaneseZenKoNoChisoku, pmc:JapaneseZenDojoFuchowa, pmc:JapaneseZenShinkyuyakuMuko, pmc:JapaneseZenHeiyuPresupposesNaikan ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-16" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-16" ;
    skos:note "Two cautions against the practice that makes the illness, both with consequences a bystander sees, the author's symptoms and the monks' approach to madness; one caution against the cure itself, that long life alone makes a corpse-guarding ghost; one objection to the method, stagnation, raised to be refused. Every failure the work names belongs either to the overstrained practice, to the rival remedies, or, for the cure, to the diligence of the practitioner. No failure is laid on the contemplation."@en .

pmp:JapaneseZenKigenSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the Yasenkanna names a term, a count or a forfeit by which the cure can be judged"@en, "Ob das Yasenkanna eine Frist, eine Zahl oder ein Pfand nennt, an dem die Heilung zu beurteilen ist"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The same work in the same witnesses, the Japanese read complete."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A term in days, months or years within which the cure or the effect is to be expected, a count of the cured, or a forfeit if it does not come."@en ;
    pm:searchTerms "In the Japanese: 五日七日, 二三七日, 期月, 三年, 三十年, 歳月, 頭を切り, 大半, 悉く, 全快, 立所に, 遲速; in the English at the same places: five to seven days, two or three weeks, a month, three years, cut off my head, almost all, the greater part, swiftly or slowly."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:JapaneseZenRosoGaKobe, pmc:JapaneseZenKoNoChisoku, pmc:JapaneseZenNaikanHeiyu, pmc:JapaneseZenTodaiNashi ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-16" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-16" ;
    skos:note "Four terms in one work and they do not agree: the monks' cure in five to twenty-one days, with the forfeit of the master's head; the hermit's own cure, most of it in less than a month; cases cured at once; and the author's own cure in a little less than three years. One count, the greater part, and no number. No term is set for the satori, the long life or the elixir; the elixir is promised after months and years."@en .

pmp:JapaneseZenShirushiSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Every sign the Yasenkanna announces for its contemplations"@en, "Jedes Zeichen, das das Yasenkanna für seine Betrachtungen ankündigt"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The same work in the same witnesses, the Japanese read complete."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "Any perception, sensation or bodily change the work tells the practitioner to expect as a mark that the contemplation is working."@en ;
    pm:searchTerms "In the Japanese: 瓠然, 鞠, 暖氣, 信, 温暖, 淸凉, 聲あり, 香氣, 輭觸, 肌膚光澤, 唯心の所現; in the English at the same places: taut, kickball, warmth, proof, cool, sound, fragrance, touch, glow."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:JapaneseZenSekaKozen, pmc:JapaneseZenDanki, pmc:JapaneseZenNansoShirushi ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-16" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-16" ;
    skos:note "Seven signs in three nodes: the taut belly of the naikan; warmth as the proof of the returning yang, quoted; and five for the butter, the sound of running water, warmth, scent, touch and glowing skin. The work says of the scent and touch that they arise because everything is made by the mind."@en .
